The problem is that sometimes CAM operations hopelessly break when the geometry is changed,
and deleting and recreating them can introduce problems.
So there is an infrastructure to check them.

The checking infrastructure in the scripts directory.
You probably want to symlink run_all_checks.py and save_cam_operations.py
to your local macro directory.
run_all_checks.py runs all scripts/check_*.py
save_cam_operations creates a snapshot of all CAM operations. It saves a few parameters, but not all!

The check scripts check the jobs against the following rules, and print out every job not conforming.

It should only identify the tool engraver001 as nonconforming.

Jobs:
	script: check_cam_operations.py
	checked: all cam operations exists, major parameters are correct
Dressuptags:
	script: check_dressuptags.py
	checked: width/height/angle=20/10/45
Profiles:
	script: check_profilings.py
	checked: final depth is 0mm
Pockets:
	script: check_pockets.py
	checked: pattern/step over=offset/50
Tools:
	script: check_tools.py
	checked: horiz feed/vert feed/spindle=10/10/100
post processor:
	script: check_outputs.py
	checked: postprocessor is marlin, and the output is jobs/<jobname>.gcode


